{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1011383-rote-bonuses-an-open-approach","id":1011383,"title":"Rote bonuses, an open approach.","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-12-02T22:59:23-08:00","id":1011383,"title":"Rote bonuses, an open approach.","content":"In my recent thread on this topic, the feedback of other people helped solidify my thoughts about fast-casting and rotes: namely that they're not all that related. The default should be dynamic magic--I really dislike the idea that it magic is +1 difficulty without a rote.<br /> <br /> I wondered aloud what sorts of bonus a rote could get instead, and I've come to the conclusion that it doesn't have to be just one thing. Rotes were always loosely defined, so it would make sense that a rote's benefits could be hashed out between players and storytellers. Rotes aren't equal. A spell a Disciple carves out of simple habit shouldn't be on the same level as one that resonates strongly with their personal interpretation of paradigm, or one that has been honed by their Tradition over generations.<br /> <br /> Minor rotes could do things like give an initiative bonus of +1 or +2. Rote rituals could take less game time per roll--or conversely, take more game time per roll and be an assured -1 difficulty for extra time.<br /> <br /> A powerful but simple rote could be cast as a reflexive action that wouldn't count against the dice pool that turn--but would still follow the rule of only one Arete action per turn. A rote could reduce the number of successes required for complicated effects, or reduce the die difficulty. The rigidity of a rote could be a buffer against Paradox, perhaps even allowing a normally vulgar effect to be treated as coincidental. A very rare and powerful rote could combine more than one of these features.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1503,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1503-faradn","user":"Faradn","date":". 12-03-2016, 02:04 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1503-faradn","name":"Faradn","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1503_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"792"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-03T02:02:50-08:00","id":1011418,"title":"","content":"I think the problem is that since Ascension isn't built for rotes to really matter mechanically, trying to make them matter is difficult without risking getting into the stupidly broken area.<br /> <br /> If rotes have variable effects... how much does it cost to learn one? Right now rotes cost nothing because they're not important enough to merit a cost. Something that lets you cast magic and perform a full unimpeded and unrelated mundane action in the same turn can't be free or it gets ridiculous.<br /> <br /> This starts to edge into a more dangerous area of having both the creative effect creation system, and then adding a rote creation system on top of that do assign costs to different boosts and so on.<br /> <br /> I think it might be easier to just look into Awakening 2e's method of handling things. Rather than trying to shove lots of different concepts under the umbrella of Rotes, it divides up such things into a few different categories that come with their own bonuses, complications, and costs.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-03T02:44:18-08:00","id":1011431,"title":"","content":"Well, it's more that by Ascension terms Awakening 2E's rotes are a type of instrument. Which they can do because awakening &quot;instruments&quot; work totally different to ascension ones.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-03T04:16:21-08:00","id":1011452,"title":"","content":"I like your approach.<br /> I use Legacy Rotes (in a nutshell: rotes with nonstandard sphere requirements).<br /> Rotes with broad applications (pretty much all examples from the sphere descriptions) should not get any bonuses.<br /> In my Mage a rote can fill the role of an instrument.<br /> Reflexive Rotes I use are essentially Counterspells.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>So, this Zen Master walks up to a hot dog stand and says: &quot;Make me one with everything!&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12022-nonsense","name":"Nonsense","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12022_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"815"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-03T06:52:48-08:00","id":1011484,"title":"","content":"Trying to come up with a viable Rote system that didn't feel like free form magick was seen as inferior is why our group completely tossed out Rotes and the Fast Casting penalty.<br /> <br /> Your ideas are cool but it's also more rules to remember. We try to go the other way and have less rules to forget.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/987-tuch","name":"Tuch","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar987_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-03T08:41:45-08:00","id":1011516,"title":"","content":"@Heavy Arms: They are free in terms of Experience, but they require game time and story reasons. One nice thing about the variable method is that the storyteller can decide how much power is included in a rote based on the factors I mentioned. No one would start the game with rotes with bonuses mentioned in my last paragraph. Many PC's would never get ones like that. But one of those more powerful benefits--more efficiency in number of successes--seems to be present in a lot of official rotes already.<br /> <br /> @Tuch: I think rotes <i>should</i> feel like part of a freeform magic system, because that's the way the game is oriented. That's why making a rigid system with &quot;rules to forget&quot; wouldn't be ideal. It would orient the game too much toward rotes. I think they should be rare, but they should be matter.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1503-faradn","name":"Faradn","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1503_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"792"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-03T10:03:42-08:00","id":1011544,"title":"","content":"My preferred approach to rotes: you spend experience to gain a rote from some sort of teacher (they cannot be self-taught, though grimoires count as teachers), and the rote then does two things for you: it lets you perform the rote whether or not you have the appropriate Spheres, and you get a dice pool that doesn't depend on your Arete. The idea is that a rote is something that you don't have to understand to use; you just have to go through the motions bringing your Focus to bear. That is, you still need some understanding of the Practice that the rote is based on (represented both by the fact that the student needs to have the rote's Practice incorporated into his Focus \u2014 you can't learn an Alchemy-based rote if you don't Practice alchemy \u2014 and by the dice pool deriving from one of the Practice's related Abilities, chosen by the teacher after taking the rote's instruments into account). Once you have enough Spheres to perform the Effect on your own, your rote knowledge provides you with a -1 \u201crote casting\u201d magical modifier. <br /> <br /> These benefits are really useful for inexperienced mages (or even Extraordinary Citizens), giving them early access to select high-level effects along with dice pools that let them be competent in their use; but the higher one's Arete and Spheres get, the less useful rotes become: by the time Arete reaches five dots, the Ability-based dice pools cease to be advantageous; and once your Sphere knowledge is sufficient let you improvise the Effect, the only thing the rote provides is a -1 difficulty modifier: better than nothing, but not by much. Basically, rotes are like training wheels. <br /> <br /> Implicit in the above is that rotes must be formalized: you must specify not just an Effect, but also a Practice, one or more instruments, and an Ability. The instruments must be used if you wish to take advantage of any of the rote's benefits, even if you have enough Arete to surpass the instrument, and of course you must use it in a way appropriate to the Practice. OTOH, you're free to use as your dice pool whichever is higher: the rote's Ability or your Arete. But if you use the Ability as your rote's pool, you don't get to use the \u201cAbilities enhancing Magick\u201d rule.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-03T12:20:27-08:00","id":1011613,"title":"","content":"Interesting idea, Dataweaver. I'm always in favor of making sorcerers even more redundant than they already are.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1503-faradn","name":"Faradn","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1503_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"792"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-03T14:03:08-08:00","id":1011655,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1011383-rote-bonuses-an-open-approach?p=1011544#post1011544\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">My preferred approach to rotes: you spend experience to gain a rote from some sort of teacher (they cannot be self-taught, though grimoires count as teachers), and the rote then does two things for you: it lets you perform the rote whether or not you have the appropriate Spheres, and you get a dice pool that doesn't depend on your Arete. The idea is that a rote is something that you don't have to understand to use; you just have to go through the motions bringing your Focus to bear. That is, you still need some understanding of the Practice that the rote is based on (represented both by the fact that the student needs to have the rote's Practice incorporated into his Focus \u2014 you can't learn an Alchemy-based rote if you don't Practice alchemy \u2014 and by the dice pool deriving from one of the Practice's related Abilities, chosen by the teacher after taking the rote's instruments into account). Once you have enough Spheres to perform the Effect on your own, your rote knowledge provides you with a -1 \u201crote casting\u201d magical modifier.<br /> <br /> These benefits are really useful for inexperienced mages (or even Extraordinary Citizens), giving them early access to select high-level effects along with dice pools that let them be competent in their use; but the higher one's Arete and Spheres get, the less useful rotes become: by the time Arete reaches five dots, the Ability-based dice pools cease to be advantageous; and once your Sphere knowledge is sufficient let you improvise the Effect, the only thing the rote provides is a -1 difficulty modifier: better than nothing, but not by much. Basically, rotes are like training wheels.<br /> <br /> Implicit in the above is that rotes must be formalized: you must specify not just an Effect, but also a Practice, one or more instruments, and an Ability. The instruments must be used if you wish to take advantage of any of the rote's benefits, even if you have enough Arete to surpass the instrument, and of course you must use it in a way appropriate to the Practice. OTOH, you're free to use as your dice pool whichever is higher: the rote's Ability or your Arete. But if you use the Ability as your rote's pool, you don't get to use the \u201cAbilities enhancing Magick\u201d rule.</div> </div> </div> </div>And by &quot;training wheels&quot;, you mean handing someone the power to generate the damage capacity of an RPG with thermobaric ammunition that never needs to be reloaded and can be fired at will? Someone who can otherwise, maybe, see auras like a mood ring or light their cigar with their thumb?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-03T17:48:32-08:00","id":1011759,"title":"","content":"What about<b></b>:<br /> <br /> - rote is not related to fast-casting, fast-casting is just that, as in M20<br /> <br /> - rotes are coded, formalized, tried-and-true spells, thus they provide a -1 difficulty bonus to cast<br /> - learning a rote is as much XP as the rote's accumulated sphere levels +1 for each additional sphere, if it uses more than one sphere. The xp represents the learning procedure.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-03T18:04:44-08:00","id":1011766,"title":"","content":"With all the ideas, approaches and things being offered back and forth, I have to ask:<br /> <br /> What problem are we trying to solve?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://is.gd/wodchart\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Probability Chart</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodcalc\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Statistics Calculator</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodarmor\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Armor System</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Damage Roll System</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage2\" target=\"_blank\">My explanation of cWoD Damage Levels</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodstrength\" target=\"_blank\">'Interesting' Strength Attribute Stuff</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodboom\" target=\"_blank\">EXPLOSIVE cWoD STUFF!</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodheresy\" target=\"_blank\">How Technocrats don't think they are Mages</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","name":"Ambrosia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6606_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1567"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-03T19:30:09-08:00","id":1011789,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ambrosia</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1011383-rote-bonuses-an-open-approach?p=1011766#post1011766\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">With all the ideas, approaches and things being offered back and forth, I have to ask:<br /> <br /> What problem are we trying to solve?</div> </div> </div> </div>That Rotes are pointed out as being really, really important but that they are vague and undefined. Said definition is inconsistent. The default setting is not really very important or useful at all with the least significant modifier possible in play. Other settings include the ability to break the rules about breaking the rules of reality in nigh earth-shattering ways. <br /> <br /> There's really nothing in the rules that would keep a player from saying &quot;Everything my character casts is a Rote. I'll take the -1 Diff on everything.&quot; If the ST wants to be (equally) pissy back, a reasonably creative player could just make up a name and BS any other info requested to describe the why's and wheretofore's of the effect's Rote-ish-ness. Hells, there's nothing to keep a player from coming in with a &quot;Random Rote Name Generator&quot; and just using it for every casting roll. After all, they can say their character had a VERY thorough education and learned literally a Rote for every occasion. <br /> <br /> There's another book coming out that is supposed to address these issues. I doubt they will actually address the concerns being raised, as they never have before, but one can hope.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-03T22:30:15-08:00","id":1011863,"title":"","content":"Ajax, I think you're discounting storyteller authority quite a bit.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1503,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1503-faradn","user":"Faradn","date":". 12-04-2016, 01:32 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1503-faradn","name":"Faradn","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1503_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"792"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-04T16:41:05-08:00","id":1012254,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Faradn</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1011383-rote-bonuses-an-open-approach?p=1011863#post1011863\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ajax, I think you're discounting storyteller authority quite a bit.</div> </div> </div> </div>You're asking a rules question, right?. What do Rotes do/what should they do? What does that -1 Diff mean? When should it be applied? Should Rotes just allow effects that wouldn't be possible with application of straight up conjunctional Sphere magic? Or allowing for effects that are seemingly impossible? Or are they just codified effects? And what does that mean?<br /> <br /> You're not asking about how, say, the characters perceive Rotes or Procedures from inside the game.<br /> <br /> If the answer is &quot;the ST decides&quot;, it's not a rules discussion, it's a game philosophy discussion. It's a &quot;What do you decide to do in your game?&quot; Really meaning, &quot;How do you arbitrarily handle this vague concept?&quot;, or, maybe, &quot;What have you and your players agreed on to handle this nigh meaningless vagueness?&quot; And Rotes seem relatively uninteresting overall from a philosophical standpoint. Particularly in Mage, where the navel-gazing tends to be about much more epic questions than &quot;Rotes: Do they even exist? And what does it mean to the universe either way?&quot; They are set up in the rules as being something that is supposed to be meaningful in the sense of game mechanics.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-04T21:00:39-08:00","id":1012368,"title":"","content":"Edit: It's hard for me to not read your tone as antagonistic. I might be wrong about that, so rather than responding antagonistically it's probably best to say I think the way you're looking at this is incorrect and leave it at that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1503,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1503-faradn","user":"Faradn","date":". 12-05-2016, 02:47 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1503-faradn","name":"Faradn","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1503_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"792"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-04T23:24:33-08:00","id":1012421,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ajax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1011383-rote-bonuses-an-open-approach?p=1011789#post1011789\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> There's really nothing in the rules that would keep a player from saying \u201cEverything my character casts is a Rote. I'll take the -1 Diff on everything.\u201c If the ST wants to be (equally) pissy back, a reasonably creative player could just make up a name and BS any other info requested to describe the why's and wheretofore's of the effect's Rote-ish-ness. Hells, there's nothing to keep a player from coming in with a \u201cRandom Rote Name Generator\u201c and just using it for every casting roll. After all, they can say their character had a VERY thorough education and learned literally a Rote for every occasion. </div> </div> </div> </div>Except that there is nothing RAW that gives you -1 difficulty for using a rote...<br /> RAW only says you receive +1 under duress/if your effect has to happen NOW.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>So, this Zen Master walks up to a hot dog stand and says: &quot;Make me one with everything!&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12022-nonsense","name":"Nonsense","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12022_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"815"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-05T07:03:23-08:00","id":1012558,"title":"","content":"I use a fairly simple house rule for Rotes: I ignore the typical +1 Diff for non-Rote Effects, and instead give a -1 Diff bonus for using a Rote. To balance this, Rotes cost 1xp per Sphere level to buy, and need to be used repeatedly, and in the same way (so as to make it rote), a story-appropriate arbitrary number of times before purchase.<br /> <br /> Additionally, I give players (especially when playing with Mage-newbies) 1-3 free Rotes upon character creation.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6384-frozenfishy","name":"Frozenfishy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6384_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"172"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-12T14:54:30-08:00","id":1016264,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ajax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1011383-rote-bonuses-an-open-approach?p=1011655#post1011655\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And by &quot;training wheels&quot;, you mean handing someone the power to generate the damage capacity of an RPG with thermobaric ammunition that never needs to be reloaded and can be fired at will? Someone who can otherwise, maybe, see auras like a mood ring or light their cigar with their thumb?</div> </div> </div> </div>That's obviously not what I mean. By \u201ctraining wheels\u201d, I mean something that has less relative utility as the mage's personal affinity for magick and mastery of the Spheres improve. But yes, if an apprentice can be taught a five-dot Rote, he can have tremendous power at his disposal. Very <i>focused</i> power (if he wants to do anything other than causing damage, he's got to learn more rotes); but power nonetheless. <br /> <br /> There are a few anti-abuse features involved here:<br /> <br /> \u00b7 First, it has to be taught; an apprentice cannot devise a two-dot rote on his own, let alone a five-dot rote. If the novice doesn't have a master who's willing to teach him the rote or access to an appropriate grimoire, he cannot learn the rote.<br /> \u00b7 Second, he must learn the rote before he can use it \u2014 that is, he must spend experience points. I didn't specify how much rotes should cost; but it would be reasonable for the more advanced rotes to require effort to learn than basic rotes would.<br /> \u00b7 Third, rotes always require the use of instruments: prevent the rote caster from using his instruments, and you prevent him from casting the rote. Some Storyteller fiat is needed for this last one, due to the fact that Storyteller fiat is needed whenever Instruments are involved; but with that in mind, I generally require more onerous Instruments for five-dot Effects than for one-dot Effects, just as a general principle: the more ambitious your effort is, the more work it should require to achieve \u2014 and not just in terms of difficulty. This holds true for masters as well as apprentices, especially where rotes are concerned.<br /> <br /> Compare this to M20's rule that Extraordinary Citizens can use Devices. In both cases, you're potentially handing tremendous power over to someone with little to no innate magickal affinity. In the case of Devices, what the mage gives the mage can also take away; as well, Devices often have restrictions (such as ammo requirements) that casting an Effect typically doesn't (though don't neglect the restrictions that instruments potentially impose). But the Sleeper gains access to the Device's Effects with minimal training (\u201cpoint this at the target, pull the trigger\u201d), as all of the work has already been done by the mage who crafted the Device. By contrast, a rote can no more easily be taken away than a memory can (though again, don't discount the instrument requirements); but it takes more effort on the part of the user to get it to work.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-12T16:21:29-08:00","id":1016302,"title":"","content":"I have no issues with the idea conceptually. It tracks with my head-space-think on how to obliterate the need for even having Sorcerers at all. They're all just Arete 0 Rote Only Mages. <br /> <br /> But, it does seem like it needs an even handed, consistent and holistic approach and, unfortunately, requires dealing with fundamentals of how Rotes work. And that's a range of mountains to climb.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-12T18:51:40-08:00","id":1016352,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Faradn</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1011383-rote-bonuses-an-open-approach?p=1011613#post1011613\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Interesting idea, Dataweaver. I'm always in favor of making sorcerers even more redundant than they already are.</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ajax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1011383-rote-bonuses-an-open-approach?p=1016302#post1016302\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I have no issues with the idea conceptually. It tracks with my head-space-think on how to obliterate the need for even having Sorcerers at all. They're all just Arete 0 Rote Only Mages.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's no accident. I started with \u201cwhat would be needed to make \u2018Arete 0 Mage\u2019 a viable option?\u201d That lead naturally to \u201cThey'd need dice pools not based on Arete\u201d (because rolling zero dice isn't going to get you anywhere) and \u201cthey'd need access to specific Effects without having Spheres\u201d (because Arete 0 would cap Spheres at zero). Thus, Rotes that can exceed your Sphere rankings and that have dice pools not based on Arete. <br /> <br /> And since I didn't want these Arete 0 proto-mages doing things that Arete 1+ mages couldn't do, it naturally followed that these special Effects would need to be available all mages. That said, I also like the notion that mages have a built-in preference for improvising Effects; so I wanted make sure that the Rotes didn't trump the standard system all the way through. Thus the built-in obsolescence.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ajax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1011383-rote-bonuses-an-open-approach?p=1016302#post1016302\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But, it does seem like it needs an even handed, consistent and holistic approach and, unfortunately, requires dealing with fundamentals of how Rotes work. And that's a range of mountains to climb.</div> </div> </div> </div> What do you think needs to be addressed that I haven't yet addressed? <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-14T03:07:32-08:00","id":1016964,"title":"","content":"I take rotes as more of a &quot;everyone knows this works good&quot; thing.<br /> Detaching it from the mage's paradigm shouldn't work, as nothing outside their paradigms works any better for starters.<br /> So that's not what rotes are good for.<br /> <br /> I go simple here. Rotes are tried and true. They're easy as far as that word matters. -1 difficulty. It probably takes a while to use, unless the magick needs to be quickly cast to be useful.<br /> The rules on how many rotes the character knows is simple. You have a few (one per sphere dot, of the related level) you can call out of your head. Any other is in an external guide, usually a grimoire, that has to be on hand during the whole casting.<br /> <br /> At ST discretion others may be learned. Unlearning a rote to give space for another requires a story behind it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/32-synapse/visitormessage/1408635-visitor-message-from-synapse#post1408635\" target=\"_blank\">Check my Exalted homebrew</a>!","edited":{"userid":32,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/32-synapse","user":"Synapse","date":". 12-14-2016, 06:12 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/32-synapse","name":"Synapse","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar32_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1507"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}